<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Paul Banyard - Full Pack Part 3</title></head><body><h1>Paul Banyard - Full Pack Part 3</h1><p>Paul Banyard — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 3)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>03-18 Pipeline Review</p><p>Plaud file id: f30b807e71466713c1e6469aba307b2e</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># Performance and Pipeline Review for Paul Banyard</p><p>This document outlines a performance review meeting for mortgage adviser Paul Banyard, led by Bob Duncan. The discussion covers an analysis of lead generation and financial metrics, a detailed review of the active case pipeline, and a strategic evaluation of Paul&#x27;s performance, concluding with targeted actions to improve protection insurance sales and maintain upward momentum.</p><p>---</p><p>## Operational and Administrative Alignment</p><p>The meeting commenced with administrative housekeeping, specifically addressing a query regarding lead management for a third-party source (&quot;the borders&quot;). Bob Duncan identified that leads were being sent via email, creating unnecessary administrative friction. To streamline this, it was decided that Paul Banyard would generate and provide a direct booking link. This allows the lead source to book appointments directly into Paul&#x27;s diary, bypassing the manual lead entry process. The team agreed this approach would improve efficiency and potentially increase conversion rates, as immediate booking reduces the likelihood of client drop-off.</p><p>Paul subsequently raised a complex client query on behalf of a colleague (Amy). The case involved a self-build property completed two years ago that lacks a formal completion certificate. The client&#x27;s neighbor had successfully remortgaged a similar property to a standard mortgage for a lower rate, prompting the inquiry. Bob Duncan advised that while lenders like Halifax might accept architectural drawings in lieu of a certificate, the absence of a completion certificate is a significant risk factor. It was agreed that the best course of action is to anonymously &quot;ping&quot; multiple lenders with the specific scenario to gauge their appetite before proceeding with a formal application.</p><p>---</p><p>## Lead Generation and Engagement Performance</p><p>Bob Duncan led a comprehensive review of Paul&#x27;s lead generation statistics, noting a distinct upward trend since the beginning of the year. While February closed with 33 leads, March is currently tracking to be on par, with a healthy mix of new inquiries and active pipeline cases. Paul confirmed that although the week started quietly, his diary has filled up rapidly with &quot;last-minute&quot; bookings—a pattern he views positively as it often correlates with higher attendance rates compared to appointments booked weeks in advance. He currently has five appointments scheduled for the immediate days ahead.</p><p>A key strategic discussion focused on internal referrals. Bob noted leads coming from specific colleagues (Jess, Kate, Gav, Sarah Tanner) and suggested Paul physically situate himself between key team members in the office to encourage further referrals. To sustain this momentum, Bob emphasized the importance of Paul providing regular, reassuring market updates to the team. Specifically, Paul was advised to contextualize recent interest rate fluctuations (0.1% - 0.5% increases) to dispel &quot;scaremongering&quot; press narratives. The objective is to arm the team with a balanced medium-to-long-term view—highlighting that unlike during COVID, lenders are adjusting pricing rather than pulling funding—which they can use to build confidence with potential clients.</p><p>---</p><p>## Workflow and Client Management Process</p><p>The discussion shifted to Paul&#x27;s management of his workflow, particularly his handling of early-stage clients. Paul explained his use of the &quot;Create Fact Find&quot; stage as a holding pen for clients who are engaged but not yet ready to transact (e.g., early renewal discussions). This prevents the system from prematurely categorizing them as &quot;appointment completed&quot; or &quot;lost.&quot; While Bob initially queried the specific workflow steps, he conceded that Paul&#x27;s method effectively mimics a &quot;keeping in touch&quot; strategy.</p><p>Bob explicitly linked Paul&#x27;s improved performance to this disciplined workflow management. By maintaining regular contact—even if just via voicemail or email—Paul keeps himself at the &quot;forefront&quot; of the client&#x27;s mind. Both Bob and Sean McLennan validated this approach, noting that previous lapses in contact often resulted in clients drifting to other brokers. The current cleanliness of Paul&#x27;s lead tracker, with very few neglected files compared to six months ago, was cited as evidence of this improved process. Bob urged Paul to continue this habit, framing it as the critical driver behind his current pipeline success.</p><p>---</p><p>## Financial Metrics and Pipeline Analysis</p><p>Paul&#x27;s financial performance was reviewed in detail, revealing a strong start to the year. He has already banked £10,000 in March, placing him in a bonus position, with a total of £20,000 banked year-to-date and a potential further £21,000 in the immediate pipeline. Bob highlighted that Paul is having a &quot;fantastic run&quot; on mortgages, with written business for March projected to hit or exceed £21,000. This represents a significant recovery and upward trajectory following a dip in November.</p><p>The analysis also touched on broader market trends affecting income. Bob noted a shift in the business mix, which is now split 50/50 between Remortgages and Product Transfers (PTs). This shift impacts revenue, as PTs typically generate only a third of the income of a full remortgage for similar effort. Despite this, Paul is on track to bridge the performance gap with top-performing peers, with Bob challenging him to aim for second place in the team rankings by closing a small £2,000–£4,000 gap in written business.</p><p>---</p><p>## Active Case Review and Protection Gap</p><p>A granular review of the active case pipeline (18 cases in progress) revealed several stalled files and urgent actions. Specific attention was drawn to a NatWest case where the lender is demanding a passport for a non-British national, despite the client&#x27;s passport being expired and in renewal. Bob instructed Paul and Sean to intervene aggressively to prevent the case from being auto-closed, which would cause the client to lose a favorable rate. Another case (Emily) suffered a down-valuation from £725k to £700k, complicating the LTV bracket; Paul needs to re-source this immediately.</p><p>The most critical finding from the review was a stark performance gap in Protection Insurance. While mortgage numbers are high, protection sales have lagged significantly this month, dragging down the overall revenue potential. Bob pointed out that &quot;protection is way down here,&quot; whereas mortgages are performing exceptionally well. It was agreed that Paul and Sean must dedicate specific time to review the 18 active mortgage cases to identify and convert missed protection opportunities, ensuring this income stream aligns with the mortgage volume.</p><p>---</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>**@Paul Banyard**</p><p>- [ ] Generate a direct booking link for &quot;the borders&quot; lead source. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Email the booking link to the team (including Amy) with instructions to book directly into the diary. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Configure the booking system to assign staff automatically to enable direct diary bookings. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Contact lenders to query policy on remortgaging a self-build property without a completion certificate. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Deliver a mortgage market update (focusing on rates vs. press narrative) to the internal team on Thursday. - [Thursday]</p><p>- [ ] Consolidate the market update into an email and send it to the team after the briefing. - [Thursday]</p><p>- [ ] Maintain &quot;keeping in touch&quot; calls/emails for early-stage clients to preserve the pipeline. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Prevent the NatWest case closure: Upload a note/PDF explaining the passport expiry issue and explicitly request the case remains open to secure the rate. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Call NatWest to forcefully instruct them &quot;do not cancel this case.&quot; - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Speak to Juliana regarding the NatWest case to see if a driving license can be accepted as alternative ID. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Re-source the &quot;Emily&quot; case following the property down-valuation. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Clarify with &quot;Emily&quot; if the baby-related purchases on bank statements are for a third party (to address lender queries). - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Schedule a dedicated session with Sean to review all 18 active cases specifically for Protection Insurance opportunities. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Ensure the rebooked protection consultation (originally for tonight) is secured for this month. - [TBD]</p><p>**@Sean McLennan**</p><p>- [ ] Call &quot;Andrea Ray&quot; to check for case updates (documents were uploaded). - [Today]</p><p>- [ ] Assist Paul in monitoring the &quot;keeping in touch&quot; clients, ensuring regular contact is maintained. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Participate in the case review session with Paul to identify missing Protection Insurance applications. - [TBD]</p><p>**@Bob Duncan**</p><p>- [ ] Reach out to John to schedule training time for Jess. - [TBD]</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p>## Weekly Adviser Performance Review — Paul Banyard  </p><p>**Content creation date:** 2026-03-18 11:43:25</p><p>Only sections with evidenced data are included.</p><p>---</p><p>## 1) Follow-Up From Previous Meeting (only actions explicitly referenced)</p><p>### Actions previously agreed</p><p>- Protection follow-ups for **Jamie Braithwaite** and **Wendy Watson**.</p><p>### Completed vs outstanding</p><p>**Completed**</p><p>- **Jamie Braithwaite** — protection follow-up completed on **2026-03-18**.  </p><p>  - **Owner:** Paul / Sean (handled on the day; exact owner not separated in transcript)</p><p>- **Wendy Watson** — protection follow-up completed on **2026-03-18**.  </p><p>  - **Owner:** Paul / Sean</p><p>**Outstanding**</p><p>- Protection appointment that was “meant to do” on **2026-03-18** — **not completed**; needs **rebooking for next week** (no date captured).  </p><p>  - **Owner:** Paul  </p><p>  - **Reason for delay:** Not completed as planned; diary/availability implied.  </p><p>  - **Risk created by inaction:** Protection remains off-pace in March; revenue mix and average case value drop.</p><p>### Behaviour patterns / ongoing blockers</p><p>- Repeated theme: when **mortgage workload increases**, **protection activity drops** (“insurance gets slightly distracted”).</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Declining** — protection actions slip when mortgage volume increases.</p><p>---</p><p>## 2) Leads Performance Review (with CRM discipline)</p><p>### A) Leads — Last Month (February)</p><p>**Current position**</p><p>- **33 leads** in February.</p><p>- Outcomes stated:</p><p>  - **8 applications** from 33 (**24.2%**) “did business with straight away”</p><p>  - **3 offers received**</p><p>  - **1 completed**</p><p>  - **4** still at **sign-up**</p><p>**Vs last week / last month**</p><p>- January totals not provided → MoM trend cannot be quantified.</p><p>**What’s working well**</p><p>- Strong immediate conversion to applications (**24.2%**).</p><p>- Evidence of real progression (offers + completion), not just enquiry volume.</p><p>**What’s not working well**</p><p>- **4 leads still at sign-up** with no client names captured → accountability/blockers not visible from transcript.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- Sign-up holding stage can go stale without dated tasks and notes → leakage risk to competitors.</p><p>- MI visibility risk without client-level clarity for the 4 sign-ups.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- None explicitly assigned for the February sign-up cohort.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- N/A (no client-level allocation captured).</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Stagnant-to-improving** — strong conversion, but unresolved sign-up cohort lacks visibility.</p><p>---</p><p>### B) Leads — This Month (March, so far)</p><p>**Current position**</p><p>- Confirmed: **1 lead signed up** month-to-date.</p><p>- Diary activity noted (bookings late in the period):</p><p>  - **7 appointments** scheduled across **2026-03-18 to 2026-03-20** (2 on 2026-03-18; 3 on 2026-03-19; 2 on 2026-03-20).</p><p>- Lead status mix referenced on-screen: **Created**, **Keeping in Contact**, **Sign Up**, **Trying to Contact**, and **2 Unable to Place**.</p><p>- Explicit CRM accuracy issue:</p><p>  - A **“created lead” last added 27 January** remains **booked in** but **not moved to “Appointment Booked”** (flagged by **Anna**).</p><p>- Lead source process issue:</p><p>  - **Borders** sending leads **manually** instead of using **direct diary booking links**.</p><p>**Vs last month (February)**</p><p>- March volume described as **roughly on par with February** at this stage (no exact MTD count provided beyond “1 signed up”).</p><p>**What’s working well**</p><p>- Lead flow described as **relatively consistent** (minor peaks/troughs).</p><p>- Late-booking is viewed as improving attendance probability (fewer bookings weeks in advance).</p><p>- Source/referral engagement broadening: leads noted from **Jess, Kate, Gav, Sarah Tanner**.</p><p>- Workflow overdue load improved overall: only **1 day overdue** item referenced (linked to travel disruption on **17 March 2026**).</p><p>**What’s not working well**</p><p>- **CRM stage hygiene failure:** “Created” lead (added **27 January**) is booked but not progressed → inaccurate MI and ownership gap (Admin).</p><p>- **Workflow fit gap:** adviser workflow lacks a clean “keeping in touch / not ready” loop → improvised staging (“create fact find” used as holding stage) and inconsistent reporting risk.</p><p>- **Lead leakage process:** Borders not using booking link → slower engagement and more chasing.</p><p>- **Unable to Place** requires clear disposition; example given:</p><p>  - **Ltd Company Buy-to-Let**, applicant **resident in Greece** (unplaceable on current criteria) — client name not captured.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- **Reporting risk:** stale stages (“Created” vs “Appointment Booked”) distorts “where are my leads?” narrative and pipeline accuracy.</p><p>- **Engagement risk:** manual lead forwarding reduces contact rate and increases no-response.</p><p>- **Drift risk:** leads in “Created / Trying to Contact” without time-bound tasks age silently.</p><p>- **Placement constraint risk:** international residency / Ltd Co BTL increases unplaceable volume unless triaged early.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- **Admin:** Move the **27 January** lead from **Created** to **Appointment Booked** and update notes.</p><p>- **Adviser:** Deliver **fortnightly market update** to WIM desk and send **follow-up email summary** after the update.</p><p>- **Adviser/Ops:** Implement **Borders** booking-link usage (direct booking into Paul’s diary) via Amy.</p><p>- **Adviser:** Review the **2 Unable to Place** leads and ensure explicit reason + closure/next steps are logged.</p><p>- **Adviser:** Audit March **Created** leads for owner, next step, and dated task (and confirm whether any are B/C grade).</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Admin (Anna):** 27 January lead stage correction.</p><p>- **Adviser (Paul):** desk update + summary email; Unable to Place disposition; Created-lead audit.</p><p>- **Other (Ops/Systems) / Amy:** booking link configuration and source compliance (Borders distribution).</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Improving** — broader sources and better diary utilisation, but **CRM accuracy + source process compliance** remain the key constraints.</p><p>---</p><p>## 3) “Where Are My Leads?” (only where evidenced)</p><p>**Current position**</p><p>- **Borders** continues sending leads **without** using the booking link, reducing booked conversations and increasing chase/non-response.</p><p>- Adviser view: conversion is stronger once spoken to (“the ones I speak to”).</p><p>**What’s working well**</p><p>- Clear lever identified: increasing **booked** calls via direct links should raise contact-to-appointment conversion.</p><p>**What’s not working well**</p><p>- **Source compliance failure:** manual forwarding persists.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- Fewer booked calls → fewer conversations → lower conversion to DIP/application.</p><p>- Misleading MI if CRM stages are inaccurate (e.g., “Created” when actually booked).</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Enforce and monitor Borders’ use of the booking link (Amy included to cascade and reinforce).</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Paul / Amy**</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Improving (pending execution)** — fix identified; outcome depends on adoption.</p><p>---</p><p>## 4) Applications in Progress (active cases mentioned)</p><p>### Portfolio overview</p><p>**Current position**</p><p>- **9 cases in progress** (noted as “quite good”).</p><p>- Team referenced **18 total cases** across Paul + Sean, with **~9 awaiting offers**.</p><p>**What’s working well**</p><p>- Strong mortgage momentum this month; several cases at “agreed” stage moving to valuation.</p><p>**What’s not working well**</p><p>- **NatWest delays** recurring across cases.</p><p>- Document churn (rejected/re-requested docs) causing resets.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- **Rate loss risk** where cases stall.</p><p>- **NatWest auto-closure risk** where “docs requested” timer expires (14-day closure discussed).</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Improving (mortgage progress)** with **process drag risk** (lender SLAs/doc churn).</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: Purchase + Remortgage — **NatWest** (Submitted **6th**; still open on **2026-03-18**)  </p><p>**Current stage**</p><p>- Application submitted; held for **ID (passport)**.</p><p>**Delays / missing documents**</p><p>- Passport required; client passport **expired** and renewal pending.</p><p>- Driving licence not accepted due to **non-British** status.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- **High risk** of NatWest **auto-closing** (14-day “docs requested” timer).</p><p>- **Rate loss risk** if it closes (new rate “significantly more expensive”).</p><p>- Dependency: **Cord** want to see **NatWest mortgage offer** before they offer.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Upload a **holding PDF note** explaining passport renewal and request case stays open.</p><p>- Instruct NatWest / **Juliana** explicitly **do not cancel**; request manual hold.</p><p>- Escalate to **Bob** if resistance from Juliana.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Sean + Paul** (Bob escalation if needed)</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Declining** — time in stage increasing with closure timer risk.</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: (Client name not captured) — Submitted **12th** — documents re-requested  </p><p>**Current stage**</p><p>- Processing; lender re-requested documents.</p><p>**Delays / missing documents**</p><p>- ID image rejected because client’s **thumb covered signature**.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- Rework loop → SLA drift and client frustration.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Confirm lender has accepted corrected ID and log next step + expected SLA in CRM.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Sean** (noted handling doc request)</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Stagnant** — progress repeatedly reset by document rejection.</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: **Angie Ray** — additional documents requested  </p><p>**Current stage**</p><p>- In progress; additional docs requested.</p><p>**Delays / missing documents**</p><p>- Correct payslip required: “last payslip before maternity leave”; Paul confirmed now **uploaded and sorted**.</p><p>- Portal showed **no update yet**; follow-up planned.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- Case can stall if lender doesn’t acknowledge receipt; missed follow-up risk if not logged.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Confirm document receipt and next timeline on **2026-03-18**; log CRM note + next task date.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Sean** (follow-up) with **Paul** oversight</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Improving** — document issue corrected, pending lender confirmation.</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: **Sarah Budge** — (Buy-to-let referenced) — offer pending valuation  </p><p>**Current stage**</p><p>- **Agreed**; offer shortly subject to satisfactory valuation.</p><p>**Key dates**</p><p>- Valuation booked for **2026-03-19**.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- Valuation delay/fail → offer delay.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Monitor valuation completion and chase offer issuance post-valuation; update CRM status at each milestone.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Sean** (Admin support if applicable)</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Improving** — clear milestone scheduled.</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: **Emily** — down-valuation + underwriting questions  </p><p>**Current stage**</p><p>- Setback (“back to square one”).</p><p>**Case details**</p><p>- Submitted **8th** aiming for valuation **£725k**.</p><p>- Valuation returned **£700k**, removing intended lower LTV route.</p><p>- Underwriter queried bank statement spend (pram/cot) → inferred baby; client hadn’t raised this.</p><p>- Must move to **80% LTV** at higher current rates (rate environment noted up ~0.5% since submission).</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- Higher pricing and dissatisfaction risk.</p><p>- Disclosure/clarity risk around future commitments/income.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Reassess route (**Product Transfer vs 80% LTV remortgage**) using current rates.</p><p>- Clarify client circumstances and respond to underwriting queries.</p><p>- Record Q&amp;A and client clarification clearly in CRM.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Paul**</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Declining** — valuation outcome removed original product route; underwriting narrative required.</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: **Siobhan** — Submitted **13th** — agreed  </p><p>**Current stage**</p><p>- **Agreed**.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- None stated beyond normal progression control.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Progress to offer; ensure milestone dates and chases are tasked in CRM.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Paul / Sean**</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Improving**.</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: **Brianna Kennedy** — **NatWest** — valuation pending  </p><p>**Current stage**</p><p>- Awaiting valuation; “everything else completed”.</p><p>**Key dates**</p><p>- Valuation booked for **2026-03-19**.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- NatWest SLA delays remain a pattern; valuation is the key dependency.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Confirm valuation completion and chase outcome/underwriting immediately after; keep CRM updated.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Sean** with **Paul** oversight</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Stagnant to improving** — ready for next gate; lender speed is variable.</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: **David Lee** — underwriting (medical records expected)  </p><p>**Current stage**</p><p>- Underwriting; expected medical records request (uncertain if already received).</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- Medical evidence delays can extend completion and commission timing.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Confirm medical records request/receipt status; set chase dates and update CRM.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Paul**</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Stagnant** — awaiting third-party evidence.</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: IP (client name not captured) — medical required  </p><p>**Current stage**</p><p>- Insurer re-review; client sent link to **book a medical**.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- Delay if client doesn’t book promptly.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Ensure client books medical; diarise chases and record updates in CRM.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Paul**</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Improving if booked quickly**; otherwise stagnates.</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: **Charlie Rintoul** — renewal / product transfer (likely **HSBC**)  </p><p>**Current stage**</p><p>- Appointment completed; next step likely HSBC product transfer, but mortgage details **cannot be pulled** due to incorrect/incomplete information held.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- Delay retrieving lender details risks delayed recommendation and dissatisfaction.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Validate/correct the data required to retrieve HSBC details; document blocker and next touchpoint in CRM.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Paul** (Admin support if needed)</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Stagnant** — cannot progress until HSBC details issue resolved.</p><p>---</p><p>### Case: **Dunlop (BNC Dunlop)** — purchase / £720,000 mortgage + protection  </p><p>**Current stage**</p><p>- Mortgage described as “big mortgage” **£720,000**; protection review due **2026-03-18** evening but **rescheduled**.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- Protection slippage → revenue timing risk and momentum loss.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Rebook protection review with confirmed date/time and log appointment + next steps in CRM.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Paul**</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Stagnant** — mortgage progressing; protection timing slipping.</p><p>---</p><p>## 5) Protection in Progress (only where evidenced)</p><p>**Current position**</p><p>- Protection performance in March described as **“slightly off pace”**; identified as the **big missing piece** this month.</p><p>- Protection opportunities referenced: **Wendy Watson**, **Jamie Braithwaite**, **Neil Bukes** (expected to bring cover to ~50% once completed).</p><p>- One protection appointment planned for **2026-03-18** not completed and needs rebooking (no date captured).</p><p>- Protection pipeline values referenced:</p><p>  - **£16k** protection written in March so far (“on already”).</p><p>  - Expected to place **~£5k** this week.</p><p>  - Additional **£3.5k** protection case pending to place/record.</p><p>**Vs last week / last month**</p><p>- Last week’s specific follow-ups (Jamie Braithwaite, Wendy Watson) were completed on **2026-03-18**.</p><p>- Month-to-month protection comparison not fully stated; manager view: protection is down vs mortgage performance in March.</p><p>**What’s working well**</p><p>- Identified cases queued and progressing (Jamie/Wendy completed; Neil Bukes planned).</p><p>- Material case value still to land (**£3.5k** pending; **~£5k** expected this week).</p><p>**What’s not working well**</p><p>- Protection activity drops when mortgage work increases.</p><p>- Rescheduling / missed appointment (2026-03-18) creating slippage risk.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- Income timing risk: cases slipping beyond month end move written business into April.</p><p>- Revenue mix risk if protection isn’t booked alongside mortgage milestones.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Place and record the **£3.5k** protection case accurately in CRM.</p><p>- Rebook and log the missed protection appointment (planned 2026-03-18; new date not captured).</p><p>- Paul + Sean to review all **18 cases** to ensure protection bookings are in place for cases at/near offer.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Paul** (placing/recording + rebooking)</p><p>- **Paul + Sean** (18-case protection booking review)</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Declining** — protection is slipping behind mortgage momentum due to focus drift and diary slippage.</p><p>---</p><p>## 6) Written Business — This Month (March) (only where evidenced)</p><p>**Current position**</p><p>- Protection written in March: **£16k** already.</p><p>- Expected to place **~£5k** this week (plus **£3.5k** referenced as pending).</p><p>**Vs same month last year**</p><p>- Chart described as tracking generally above prior year recently; no exact March-last-year figure stated.</p><p>**What’s working well**</p><p>- Strong written momentum; forecast suggests further increase within March.</p><p>**What’s not working well**</p><p>- Discrepancy in recollection (“five-ish vs four-ish”) indicates need for single source of truth in CRM/MI.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- If not promptly placed/recorded, written totals become disputable and forecasting credibility drops.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- Confirm exact protection amount placed and ensure CRM reflects correct figure and case notes.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- **Paul**</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Improving** — written levels building, provided cases are placed/recorded promptly.</p><p>---</p><p>## 7) Banked Business — This Month (March) (only where evidenced)</p><p>**Current position**</p><p>- **£10k banked in February**.</p><p>- **£10k banked so far in March**.</p><p>- Adviser is **already in bonus**.</p><p>**Vs last year**</p><p>- No March last-year banked figure provided.</p><p>**What’s working well**</p><p>- Strong banked consistency across February and March-to-date.</p><p>**What’s not working well**</p><p>- No evidenced banking blockers stated.</p><p>**Risks / blockers**</p><p>- General slippage risk if pipeline milestones and protection activity aren’t controlled.</p><p>**Actions required**</p><p>- None explicitly assigned.</p><p>**Action owner**</p><p>- N/A</p><p>**Trend insight:** **Improving/stable at a high level** — consistent banked performance maintained.</p><p>---</p><p>## 8) Renewal / Remortgage Income (including “Where are my renewals?” activity)</p><p>**Current position**</p><p>- **6** renewals booked in or attempted to be booked in (end-of-year context referenced).</p><p>- Tracker: **4 renewals**, plus **1 rental** to add (= **5**), with possibility of **another missed** entry.</p><p>**Vs last week / last month**</p><p>- “Probably not a lot will have changed since we last spoke” —